CloudBurrow patch 95 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1784). Applies after patch 0090.

BigQuery reads a name that gives no project in the project of the query,
and a name of one part in its default dataset
(https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/lexical#table_names).
The catalog held every object of every project in one root catalog, under
every suffix of its path ("project.dataset.name", "dataset.name", "name"),
the first object made under a name keeping it (existsTable,
existsFunction, existsTVF). So in a query of project B, `ds.t`, `ds.f(x)`,
`ds.tvf()`, `ds.t_*` and `ds.INFORMATION_SCHEMA.TABLES` named project A's
objects when A had made them first: B read A's rows and ran A's function,
and INFORMATION_SCHEMA listed both projects' tables of a dataset ID.

An object of a project's path (three parts, projectScoped) is now
registered in the root catalog under its own path alone ("p.ds.t", and
the sub-catalogs p, then ds, which hold "ds.t" and "t"); the aliases
without the project go to a catalog of their own (Catalog.aliases). A
statement is analyzed in lookupCatalog: for a connection of a project, a
MultiCatalog of the project's sub-catalog, its default dataset's and the
root catalog; for a connection with no name path (googlesqlite's own
tests), the root catalog and the aliases. A wildcard path is merged with
the name path and matches the tables of its project and dataset by their
paths; an INFORMATION_SCHEMA view's vtab filter is "project.dataset".
A view's and a SQL function's body are formatted when they are made, so
they keep the names of their own project.

The regression test is names_of_the_project_test.go.

